  "response": "<solution>\nimport sys\nfrom bisect import bisect_left, bisect_right\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all tokens from standard input\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    N = int(input_data[0])\n    Q = int(input_data[1])\n    \n    # Extract the sequence A\n    A = [int(x) for x in input_data[2:N+2]]\n    \n    # Store queries grouped by R_i\n    queries = [[] for _ in range(N + 1)]\n    idx = N + 2\n    for i in range(Q):\n        R = int(input_data[idx])\n        X = int(input_data[idx+1])\n        queries[R].append((X, i))\n        idx += 2\n        \n    ans = [0] * Q\n    \n    # dp[l] will store the minimum ending element of an increasing subsequence of length l + 1\n    dp = []\n    \n    # Process elements of A one by one\n    for i in range(N):\n        val = A[i]\n        \n        # Find the position to replace or append in the DP array\n        pos = bisect_left(dp, val)\n        if pos == len(dp):\n            dp.append(val)\n        else:\n            dp[pos] = val\n        \n        # Answer all queries that end exactly at the current prefix R = i + 1\n        for X, q_idx in queries[i + 1]:\n            # The maximum length of an increasing subsequence with all elements <= X\n            # is the number of elements in the dp array that are <= X.\n            ans[q_idx] = bisect_right(dp, X)\n            \n    # Print the answers in the original order of queries\n    print('\\n'.join(map(str, ans)))\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    solve()\n</solution>\n\nConfidence level: High. The properties of the standard $O(N \\log N)$ LIS algorithm inherently allow querying the length of the longest increasing subsequence bounded by a maximum value $X_i$ simply by doing a binary search (`bisect_right`) on the DP array. Sorting the queries offline avoids recomputing prefixes. No uncertain edge cases.",
